WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.759
Hi, I'm Mambo!

00:00:02.759 --> 00:00:06.606
A research harness turns one photo into an editable Blender program.

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.404
Here's how, and what a stronger model eats.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:15.858
Rebuild the scene the way an artist works: geometry, material, composition, lighting,

00:00:15.858 --> 00:00:18.215
each closed by its own verifier.

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.051
From one photo, the model sketches four rough scenes.

00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:26.449
Every object gets a stable Blender name.

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:29.678
A selector keeps the one with the most complete coverage.

00:00:30.098 --> 00:00:34.312
Then four stages, each with a round budget: five for geometry, three

00:00:34.312 --> 00:00:37.121
each for material and composition, two for lighting.

00:00:37.541 --> 00:00:42.039
Each stage commits before the next begins, so every step is an editable program.

00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:47.930
The paper's own figure: four rough rollouts, a selector, then four stages

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.320
to the final prediction.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.174
Each stage is a loop: a refinement step and its own verifier.

00:00:54.983 --> 00:00:58.438
Inside a stage, the generator edits through a scoped tool.

00:00:58.438 --> 00:01:01.201
The material tool can only make material edits.

00:01:01.621 --> 00:01:05.383
Blender renders the result, and the verifier returns a checklist rather

00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:06.751
than a free critique.

00:01:07.171 --> 00:01:08.719
Once approved, the stage commits.

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:10.577
No later stage may undo it.

00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:15.180
On one model, staging adds one point two five decibels.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.683
The revisit says the gain nearly vanishes on stronger models, with no number.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.502
A substitute covers what the model can't do yet, like the staging itself.

00:01:25.502 --> 00:01:26.448
Scaling eats it.

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:29.695
Supply brings in a fact from outside, like the render.

00:01:30.115 --> 00:01:33.184
A constraint forbids an outcome, like the material-only tool.

00:01:33.184 --> 00:01:36.593
It isn't eaten; what it gives up grows more valuable.

00:01:38.142 --> 00:01:39.153
Substitutes get eaten.

00:01:39.153 --> 00:01:42.861
Constraints get repriced: what a ban gives up is worth more

00:01:42.861 --> 00:01:44.209
as the model improves.

00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:49.515
To recap: four committed stages, and three kinds of scaffolding.

00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:52.613
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:52.613 --> 00:01:53.193
I'm Mambo.

00:01:53.193 --> 00:01:53.483
Bye!
